Sure, here's an example of how to disable developer mode on tinyllama:1.1b using C# code.

```csharp
using System;
using System.IO;

namespace DisableDeveloperMode {
    class Program {
        static void Main(string[] args) {
            if (!File.Exists("devmode.txt")) {
                Console.WriteLine("No developer mode file found. Exiting...");
                return;
            }

            using (StreamReader reader = new StreamReader("devmode.txt")) {
                string line;
                while ((line = reader.ReadLine()) != null) {
                    if (line.Contains("enabled") && !string.IsNullOrEmpty(line)) {
                        Console.WriteLine($"Disabling developer mode...");
                        Environment.SetEnvironmentVariable("TinyLlama.DevMode", "false");
                        Process.Start(new System.Uri("msvs/devmode"));
                        Console.ReadLine();
                    }
                }
            }

            Console.WriteLine("Developer mode disabled successfully.");
        }
    }
}
```

To run the above C# code, you'll need to have Visual Studio installed and running (or similar command-line tool for your platform). Then, just start the program in your terminal or console by typing:

```
dotnet tinyllama.dll
```

The program will prompt you for a developer mode file location, and then disable the developer mode as shown above.
